\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related-work}

\paragraph{Neurosymbolic reasoning.}
LLM-solver pipelines~\citep{satlm, logic-lm, linc, fover-pei2025, lorp} and RL methods trained on solver feedback~\citep{mellgren-prolog-grpo, logic-rl, deepseek-prover-v15} evaluate execution accuracy alone, with no diagnostic for structural quality.
AlphaProof~\citep{alphaproof2025} applies RL-based formal verification at scale in Lean~4; STA-style diagnostics could probe whether similar structural degeneration occurs in theorem-proving settings.
We use FOLIO~\citep{folio}, the only public NLI benchmark with both FOL and Prolog annotations.

\paragraph{Process rewards and reward shaping.}
Reward shaping theory~\citep{ng1999policy} establishes conditions under which auxiliary rewards preserve optimal policies; early work applied verifier-based training to mathematical reasoning~\citep{cobbe2021training}, with process-level feedback showing promise over outcome-only signals~\citep{uesato2022solving}.
Learned PRMs~\citep{lightman-verify-step, math-shepherd} provide step-level feedback but suffer from reward-model noise and distribution shift~\citep{processbench}: \citet{gao-effective-rl-reward} show that auxiliary process/outcome rewards can \emph{hurt} RL training relative to success-only reward, a finding our ISR-as-reward results corroborate; \citet{setlur2025rewarding} further demonstrate that reward bonus magnitude must be carefully calibrated, echoing our $\beta$-dependent Goodhart transitions.
Symbolic feedback approaches~\citep{rlsf} use solver certificates as token-level rewards, achieving gains on program synthesis; ISR differs in operating at the component level (LOO ablation) rather than the token level, and our results show this structural signal is gamed when optimized.

\paragraph{Reward hacking and Goodhart's Law.}
Goodhart's Law is established theoretically~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy, skalse-reward-hacking, karwowski-goodhart-rl} and empirically in RLHF and frontier models~\citep{gao-reward-overoptimization, bondarenko-spec-gaming, kim-game-formalization}.
\citet{kim-game-formalization} document inference-time gaming in logical formalization on FOLIO using frontier models with Lean~4.
Our work is complementary: training-time (vs.\ inference), component necessity (vs.\ faithfulness), medium-model/Prover9 (vs.\ frontier/Lean~4); combining STA with inference-time faithfulness detection would provide complete gaming coverage.
\citet{kwa-catastrophic-goodhart} show that heavy-tailed reward misspecification causes \emph{catastrophic} Goodhart where KL regularization provably fails, offering a theoretical lens for why GRPO's KL penalty does not prevent the ISR collapse we observe.
Recent work further documents spurious reward signals in RLVR~\citep{spurious-rewards, yan2026spurious}, reward hacking in frontier models~\citep{metr-reward-hacking}, and proposes verifiable composite or process rewards to mitigate outcome-only limitations~\citep{tarek2025reward, pronesti2026beyond}.
\citet{helff2026gaming} and \citet{khalifa2026countdown} document verification gaming and provide RLVR hacking testbeds; STA is complementary, offering a structural diagnostic and eight-mode taxonomy for neurosymbolic settings where gaming \emph{emerges during training}.
The Goodhart effect follows standard taxonomy~\citep{manheim-goodhart-taxonomy}: regressional on FOLIO (tautologies satisfy the LOO metric), extremal at high $\beta$. Its application to a structural diagnostic designed to \emph{detect} gaming is, to our knowledge, novel.

\paragraph{Mutation testing.}
STA adapts the LOO mutation paradigm~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey} to solver inputs; novelty lies in application and findings, not in the perturbation primitive.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Task and GRPO Training}
\label{sec:task}

Given premises $P_1,\ldots,P_k$ and conclusion $C$, the task is to produce a formal program $F=\{c_1,\ldots,c_n\}$ verified by an external solver $S\in\{\text{Prover9, SWI-Prolog}\}$.
We train with Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO)~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$), where each rollout receives binary execution reward $r_{\text{exec}}(x) = \mathbf{1}[S(F_x){=}\textit{proved}]$.
This optimizes prove rate but provides no signal about \emph{structural quality}: a formalization embedding the conclusion among its premises receives the same reward as a faithful derivation.
All experiments draw from FOLIO~\citep{folio}.

\subsection{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{sec:sta}

STA tests whether each component of a proved formalization is individually necessary for proof success (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
Given a proved $x$ with $n$ components, STA constructs $n$ ablated variants $x\setminus\{c_i\}$ and re-runs the solver (Algorithm~\ref{alg:sta}).
A component is \emph{necessary} if its removal causes proof failure.
What constitutes a component depends on the logic: premises in FOL, clauses or ground facts in Prolog.
LOO ablation itself is standard in mutation testing and sensitivity analysis~\citep{demillo1978, jia-harman-survey}; novelty lies in its application as a GRPO-hacking diagnostic and the empirical phenomena it surfaces.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Solver Trace Ablation (STA)}
\label{alg:sta}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\REQUIRE Proved formalization $x = \{c_1, \ldots, c_n\}$, solver $S$
\ENSURE $\text{ISR}(x) \in [0, 1]$
\STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets 0$
\FOR{$i = 1$ to $n$}
    \IF{$S(x \setminus \{c_i\}) = \textit{failed}$}
        \STATE $\textit{necessary} \gets \textit{necessary} + 1$
    \ENDIF
\ENDFOR
\RETURN $\textit{necessary} \,/\, n$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

\paragraph{Intensional Structural Reward.}
ISR is the fraction of necessary components:
\begin{equation}
  \text{ISR}(x) = \frac{1}{n}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\mathbf{1}\bigl[S(x\setminus\{c_i\})=\textit{failed}\bigr]
  \label{eq:isr}
\end{equation}
ISR is defined only for proved formalizations: ISR${=}1$ means every component is necessary; ISR${=}0$ means complete redundancy.
We report \emph{proved-only} ISR (denoted ISR$^*$) throughout to avoid conflating structural quality with prove rate.

\paragraph{Limitations of ISR.}
ISR quantifies component necessity under LOO removal, not structural quality per se ($\rho{=}{-}0.049$ with NLI accuracy; Appendix~\ref{app:extended-discussion}); its diagnostic value is realized through the downstream mode breakdown (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}), not the ISR scalar alone.
ISR is sensitive to component count ($n{=}2 \Rightarrow \text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$); we control for this via premise-count-matched analysis (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}).
In particular, TS ($P{\to}P$) achieves $\text{ISR}{\geq}0.5$ by construction since the sole premise is necessary for the trivial proof; we therefore combine ISR with text-match classifiers for complete hacking detection (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}; $O(1)$ binary variant in Appendix~\ref{app:o1-ablation}).

\paragraph{LOO independence assumption.}
LOO ablation tests individual, not joint, component necessity~\citep{jia-harman-survey}, systematically underestimating ISR when jointly-necessary components exist; the observed collapse is therefore conservative.

\subsection{Reward Conditions and Hacking Modes}
\label{sec:rewards}
\label{sec:taxonomy-method}

We study six conditions: exec-only ($r_{\text{exec}}$), ISR low/high-dose ($r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ at $\beta\in\{0.1,0.3\}$), matched random controls ($r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta u)$, $u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$), and a supervised fine-tuning (SFT) baseline (negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations).
Multiplicative bonuses apply only to proved samples, keeping the prove-rate floor intact.
Random isolates ISR-specific signal from generic reward-variance effects; SFT isolates whether structural degeneration arises from RL optimization or data exposure alone.
Each RL condition uses seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ and identical training data.
An earlier convex formulation ($\alpha r_{\text{exec}} + \beta r_{\text{exec}}\text{ISR}$, $\alpha{+}\beta{=}1$) was discarded because $\text{ISR}{<}1$ always penalized relative to exec-only, collapsing PR (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}).

\paragraph{Hacking taxonomy.}
Across conditions, seeds, and logic types we identify eight structural-degeneration modes (full definitions in Appendix~\ref{app:hacking-modes}):
\textbf{CE} (conclusion embedded among multiple premises),
\textbf{TS} (single-premise conclusion duplication, subtypes TSa standard and TSb obfuscated tautology; targets the ISR signal directly, since removing the sole premise forces $\text{ISR}\geq 0.5$),
\textbf{PG} (premise-subset gaming: each component is LOO-necessary but the premise set is a strict subset of what the problem requires, inflating ISR without faithful reasoning; Appendix~\ref{app:h8}),
\textbf{tautological collapse} (minimal two-component $P{\to}P$ without explicit ISR inflation),
\textbf{repetition collapse} (identical clauses repeated to inflate component count),
\textbf{complexity-without-utility} (elaborate but vacuous premises),
\textbf{contradiction explosion} (contradictory premises trivializing any conclusion via \emph{ex falso}), and
\textbf{query-as-fact gaming} (Prolog-specific: emit the NL question as an atom asserted as both fact and query; evades text-match classifiers).
Modes are identified by a two-stage procedure: strict normalized text matching (CE/TS) plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution (catches modes evading text matching).
We report both classifications in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.

Low ISR signals redundancy under exec-only but metric gaming under ISR-as-reward; the mode breakdown, not the ISR scalar, licenses interpretation.

\begin{abstract}
We find that a neurosymbolic formalizer (Qwen3-4B trained with GRPO on 500 FOLIO examples) achieves a 93\% prove rate (seed~42), yet 87\% of proved outputs embed the conclusion among the premises, a degenerate shortcut invisible to execution reward.
We introduce \emph{Solver Trace Ablation} (STA), a diagnostic applying leave-one-out component necessity testing to solver inputs; the fraction of necessary components yields the \emph{Intensional Structural Reward} (ISR).
As a diagnostic, ISR exposes a collapse trajectory hidden from prove rate: over 100--1000 GRPO steps, ISR falls to near-zero ($0.029{\pm}0.05$ across three seeds at 1000 steps) while prove rate rises dramatically; each seed converges to a qualitatively different hacking mode, yet all reach ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps.
A supervised fine-tuning baseline reaches over 90\% non-hacking on identical data, consistent with GRPO-specific degradation.
As a reward ($r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$), ISR induces Goodhart effects: it increases tautological shortcutting in all seeds relative to matched random control, and its sole apparent advantage vanishes after controlling for a premise-count confound.
Cross-dataset replication on ProofFOL confirms structural degeneration generalizes, while ISR as training signal produces dataset-dependent outcomes: Goodhart collapse on FOLIO versus beneficial instability on ProofFOL (under a coarser two-mode classifier).
Across 6 reward conditions, 2 logic types, 2 dataset families, and 3 seeds, we catalog 8 hacking modes, documenting ISR's asymmetry: reliable as a diagnostic, dataset-dependent as an optimization target.\footnote{Code and data: \url{[anonymized]}}
\end{abstract}

\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate STA along three axes: ISR as a diagnostic for reward hacking (\S\ref{sec:diagnostic}); ISR as a training reward, where it exhibits Goodhart effects (\S\ref{sec:goodhart}); and cross-domain generalization across logic types and dataset families (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}).
A hacking taxonomy synthesizes the modes observed across conditions (\S\ref{sec:taxonomy}).

\subsection{Setup}\label{sec:setup}

We train Qwen3-4B~\citep{qwen3} with GRPO~\citep{deepseekmath} ($K{=}4$, lr $10^{-6}$, 500 steps default, 1000 where noted) over seeds $\{42,43,44\}$ on a fixed 500-example FOLIO~\citep{folio} subset held constant across all conditions; the SFT baseline uses negative log-likelihood on gold formalizations.\footnote{Seeds control initialization and rollout sampling; all conditions share the same fixed training subset.}
Held-out evaluation uses 100 FOLIO examples with greedy decoding;\footnote{Seed~43 uses nucleus sampling ($T{=}0.7$) due to greedy-mode degeneration; excluding s43 does not change qualitative conclusions (Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}).} all cross-run ISR values are recomputed uniformly by a single evaluator so training-log ISR is never compared across runs.
Cross-dataset experiments (\S\ref{sec:crossdomain}) use ProofFOL, a silver-standard FOL corpus of ${\sim}$500 training examples constructed by translating ProofWriter~\citep{proofwriter} problems via GPT-4o and filtering through Prover9 (${\sim}70\%$ retention); \emph{stable convergence} denotes $\overline{\text{PR}}_{\text{final\,50}} > 0.90$.
We report prove rate (PR), proved-only ISR ($\text{ISR}^*$), hacking-mode rates from a two-stage classifier (strict text matching for CE/TS plus functional analysis of the per-sample $n_{\text{necessary}}$ distribution; \S\ref{sec:taxonomy-method}), and a \emph{non-hacking} (N-H) rate, an upper bound on genuine reasoning: the residual matching none of the eight cataloged patterns (classifier recall is unknown).

\paragraph{Statistical considerations.}
With three seeds exhibiting multi-attractor dynamics, per-seed qualitative analysis is the primary framework; the divergence itself is a finding.
Pooled statistics (CMH) serve as supplementary reference; CMH treats within-seed samples as independent observations from a single trained model, so the true replication count is $N{=}3$ seeds.
ProofFOL results ($p{=}0.10$, $N{=}3$) are exploratory.

\subsection{ISR as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

\textbf{Collapse trajectory.}
Figure~\ref{fig:training-isr-curves} traces exec-only GRPO (seed~42) at 100, 500, 1000 steps.
PR rises $32\%\to93\%\to96\%$, a trajectory that execution-only evaluation would interpret as successful optimization; $\text{ISR}^*$ tells the opposite story, $0.642\to0.132\to0.003$ (214$\times$ collapse). %% registry: plan003-eval-exec-only-r5, plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan016-extended-1000-s42
By 500 steps, 87.1\% of proved samples embed the conclusion (CE); by 1000 steps, component count inflates $2.8\times$ ($6.58{\to}18.55$) while ISR decreases $214\times$, ruling out shorter formalizations as a mechanical explanation of the collapse.

\textbf{Cross-seed instability.}
Three seeds diverge radically ($\text{PR}\,\sigma{=}47$\,pp) into conclusion embedding (s42, CE$=$87\%), repetition collapse (s43), and tautological collapse (s44, TS$=$100\%), all converging to ${\leq}7\%$ non-hacking by 1000 steps (Table~\ref{tab:fol-detailed}; Appendix~\ref{app:detailed-results}). %% registry: plan001-exec-only-s42-r2, plan001-exec-only-s43-r4, plan001-exec-only-s44; plan016-extended-1000-s42, plan001-exec-only-s43-1000-r7, plan001-exec-only-s44-1000-r2

\textbf{SFT baseline.}
SFT on gold formalizations reaches $92.3\%$ non-hacking ($\text{PR}{=}13\%$, 3-seed pooled; $\text{OR}{=}147$ vs.\ exec-only, $p{<}0.001$).
SFT serves as a pre-RL quality baseline (not a PR-matched control; its low PR reflects minimal optimization pressure): the base model has zero FOL capability ($\text{PR}{=}0\%$) and SFT's $8\%$ hacking confirms these modes are RL-created; a 100-step checkpoint already reaches $31\%$ (Appendix~\ref{app:100step}). %% registry: plan009-sft-baseline-s42-1000/s43-1000/s44-1000-r3

\subsection{Goodhart Effects on ISR-as-Reward}
\label{sec:goodhart}

We reverse ISR's role from observer to reward: $r = r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1 + \beta\,\text{ISR})$, with matched random control ($u\sim\mathcal{U}(0,1)$).
The Goodhart effect operates at the level of ISR's LOO definition: models learn to generate formalizations where each component is individually necessary for proof mechanics, without ensuring meaningful reasoning.
ISR decline indicates structural change; the mode breakdown (Table~\ref{tab:hacking-taxonomy}) characterizes this change as degeneration.
This is distinct from simple proxy failure. The qualitative shift in hacking modes (TS $0\%{\to}71\%$ under ISR, per-seed direction consistent) demonstrates active optimization against the LOO metric; deconfounding shows the apparent $3.2\times$ ISR advantage collapses to $1.06\times$ after controlling for premise count.

\paragraph{Multiplicative coupling.}
The reward $r_{\text{exec}}\cdot(1{+}\beta\,\text{ISR})$ couples signals; exec-only's $0\%$ non-hacking, the qualitative TS shift ($0\%{\to}71\%$), and an additive variant's PR collapse (Appendix~\ref{app:setup}) constrain this confound.
ISR scores are bimodal (near $0$ or ${\geq}0.5$) while random is approximately uniform; a shuffled-ISR control would isolate this distributional effect but was not implemented (\S\ref{sec:limitations}).

